        "title": "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "5c19ea88-4652-5feb-b49c-a5fe7156da75",
        ":question/problem": "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{16}}=1?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{5}{16}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{5}{16},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
